---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import OresCode from "../components/OresCode.astro";

import { languageMotionExamples as motion } from "../data/language-motion-examples.mjs";
const { pipelineCode, mlCode, httpCode, httpStreamingCode, selectCode } = motion;

const dataCode = `
define interface HasName as
  name: string;
end

type HN = HasName;

fnc findName(): Option<String> {
  const user = infer struct {
    name: "Ada",
    enabled: true
  };

  match user over
    on structural HN as named -> {
      return Option.some(named.name);
    }
    on _ -> {
      return Option.none();
    }
  end
}
`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AttoLang Examples"
  description="Illustrative AttoLang actor pipelines, AI/ML backpropagation, typed HTTP handlers, select statements, and structural matching."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Examples</p>
    <h1>Syntax that exposes the runtime model.</h1>
    <p>These design examples illustrate typed actor pipelines, machine-learning training, HTTP handling, and selection. They are highlighted with the site’s AttoLang tokenizer.</p><p>Design sketches only: these snippets have not been compiler-verified. Empty pipeline actors show topology only; the ML and HTTP APIs are proposed; see <a href="/language/">language verification status</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="code-stack">
      <OresCode caption="Three actors, one typed pipeline" code={pipelineCode} />
      <OresCode caption="Actor-based AI/ML training with backpropagation (design sketch)" code={mlCode} />
      <OresCode caption="Unary HTTP actor: Fetch-style response (proposed)" code={httpCode} />
      <OresCode caption="HTTP body streaming and trailers (proposed)" code={httpStreamingCode} />
      <p>The proposed <code>HttpResponse</code> has Fetch-style status, headers, and a readable body stream. The HTTP runtime sends the stream with backpressure after <code>run</code> returns; trailers are an AttoLang server-side extension, not part of the WHATWG <code>Response</code> API. Neither example is compiler-verified.</p>
      <OresCode caption="Select one ready channel operation" code={selectCode} />
      <p>The three intended forms are <code>select {}</code> (suspending result expression), <code>do select {}</code> (suspending statement), and <code>nb select {}</code> (asynchronous expression returning a Future). Nonblocking select is not a synchronous poll. Legacy combined prefixes are not canonical. Result-mode support remains a design target across backends.</p>
      <OresCode caption="Structural shapes and option-safe returns (design sketch)" code={dataCode} />
      <p>A structural pattern can match a record, struct, or class by accessible field names and compatible types, even with additional fields. Use <code>~ &#123;id: string, active: bool&#125; as a</code> as shorthand for <code>structural &#123;id: string, active: bool&#125; as a</code>. The <code>as</code> binder is local to its match arm; private or actor-sealed state remains inaccessible. This is a proposed semantic contract, not compiler-verified support. See <a href="https://github.com/ores-truffle-oreslang/ores-truffle-oreslang.github.io/blob/main/docs/STRUCTURAL_MATCH_DESIGN.md">structural matching design</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><a href="/#examples">← Back to the Examples section on the home page</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
